/* Strategic command: set the route to a named station or solar system.
 *
 * Success means the route panel carries markers. That is also the first
 * condition, which makes the command idempotent: a route already set is a
 * command already done, and a mission restarted mid-flight does not throw
 * away the route the ship is following.
 *
 * The steps below are the client's own order, learned the hard way: the
 * magnifier is a toggle, the panel keeps its region in the tree even when
 * closed, and the results window buries the wanted category under hundreds of
 * character names until the groups are collapsed. */

-> set_route {
    ? {
        /* Already there: nothing to do. */
        is_route_set?()

        -> {
            /* Nothing to look for — the mission was given no destination. */
            set_route_has_destination?(destination)
            @! is_session_change?()

            /* A memory sequence, and it has to be one. A fallback would end
               at the first step that succeeds — opening the panel, and the
               command would call itself done having typed nothing. A plain
               sequence would re-run every step each tick, and two of these
               steps undo each other: collapsing the groups is done when no
               category is expanded, expanding one is done when a category
               is, so the pair would fight forever. Memory keeps the finished
               steps finished. */
            ->* {
                set_route_open_search()
                set_route_clear_field()
                set_route_type_destination(destination)
                set_route_collapse_groups()
                set_route_expand_category(destination_kind)
                set_route_choose(destination)
            }
        }
    }
}
